Subject: [PATCH 10761/11362] Evidence for the id-range hypothesis, plus a test
 that settles it next milestone

Milestone 602's YAML held 4 tasks, exp6874 through 6877, while its document claimed
experiment_range 6874 to 6884, eleven ids. Milestone 603 has just activated with 4
tasks numbered 6885 to 6888 -- starting immediately after 6884, the end of 602's
reserved range, not after 6877, the last id actually used.

So the allocator advanced by the reserved width rather than the used count, which is
consistent with a reservation the planner then underfilled. That upgrades yesterday's
hypothesis from a guess to something supported by the id allocation, while still not
confirmed in code.

The entry records a test rather than leaving this to be re-investigated. 603's first
task, exp6885-v603-executable-manifest-branch-contract, is the structural analogue of
exp6874 and runs the same parity check. If 603's document also claims a wider range
than its 4 tasks, exp6885 fails v603_document_yaml_parity and cascade-blocks 6886,
6887 and 6888 exactly as 602 did, making it a second occurrence. If exp6885 passes,
602 was specific and the id-range reading is wrong or incomplete.

Recorded against the earlier base rate of one parity failure across five replans, so
a second occurrence would materially change it.

